rs, old routers, phones, and servers get replaced, and new ones support IPv6 by default. This works, but at geological speed. 3. **Hope that the last holdouts (legacy enterprises, small ISPs, embedded systems) will either die or be compelled by market forces.** That hope is thin. ### The real failure: No one was held accountable. The internet's governing bodies (IETF, ICANN, RIRs) assumed that once IPv4 ran out, the market would automatically switch. They didn't create strong incentives or penalties. They didn't mandate a sunset date for IPv4 on public networks. They left it to "voluntary adoption," which is another word for "kicking the can." ### What would a real plan look like? A real plan would involve: - A firm, decade‑out date after which no new IPv4 allocations are allowed (already happened). - A global price floor or tax on IPv4 addresses to make them more expensive than IPv6. - Mandatory IPv6 support for all government procurement. - Financial support for small ISPs to upgrade. - Penalties for large content providers that fail to serve IPv6 users. None of this exists. Instead, we have a free‑market scramble that punishes the unprepared. ### So where does that leave us? You're right: IPv6 on paper is meaningless. The plan is a slow, ugly, inequitable mess. The internet will not collapse, but it will become more centralized, more expensive for small players, and less open. The "solution" is a fantasy we tell ourselves to avoid admitting we failed to coordinate. The only honest answer: **There is no working plan. We're all just hoping the patches hold long enough for the old hardware to die.**
